WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.000
Normally, when I’m filming
somewhere like this,

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.560
I try and set the camera so
it's got that kind-of

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.920
professional, depth-of-field...

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.880
you know the shot, everything in
the background is blurred.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.560
But there's a windmill back there,

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.760
and I really want that in focus.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.800
I had to throw out nearly
all my script for this video.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.080
Normally, when I go
and film somewhere,

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.680
I try and write everything
in advance.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.840
I research things in detail
before I get there,

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.800
when I arrive somewhere
there should be no surprises.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.560
But I knew, even as I
set up for filming

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.480
on the roof of
Royal FloraHolland,

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.240
that perhaps this video
wasn't going to go to plan.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.680
Good morning, it's just after sunrise here
in Aalsmeer, in the Netherlands.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.160
This is one of the largest commercial
buildings in the world,

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.160
and today, just like every weekday,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.280
millions of fresh flowers are being
bought and sold inside. Sort of.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.920
This market has existed is some form
for a hundred years,

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.240
and recently there have been some changes.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.080
Because here's how the story's
supposed to go:

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.080
the auction room will be full of buyers,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.320
frantically pushing buttons to bid as
cart after cart after cart after cart after cart

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.320
of fresh flowers are paraded
in front of them

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.880
in a massive, perfectly-timed
logistics operation.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.600
But I’m not sure that
happens any more.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.920
All the English-language sources
I found are out of date,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.280
because things are changing
at the Dutch flower market.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.960
It turns out a lot of the Dutch-language
sources are out of date too.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:16.560
The truth is that a famous
decades-old tradition has ended,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.040
in the same way that video-rental stores
gave way to streaming.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.800
A thing that tourists came to see,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.600
and that a lot of people
think still happens… is no more.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.600
So I’m going to update the
English-speaking world:

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.120
the famous Dutch Flower Auction Clock
is no more.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.760
But the flower market that it was
once part of is still there,

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.960
and it runs on an almost
incomprehensible scale.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:36.399
IN DUTCH:
 
 
 

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.080
Efficiency. We’ll come back
to that shortly.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.040
I often complain that the camera doesn’t
capture the scale of something that I visit,

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.720
but in this case, it’s not just the camera:

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.080
it’s that there are no good sightlines
anywhere in that warehouse.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.240
It’s next to the airport,
so I can’t get drone shots.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.480
And it’s not that tall,
so there’s no one point

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.160
where you can overlook
the whole thing.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.680
Look, it’s so big that the employees
sometimes use bikes inside

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:40.960
to get from one end to the other.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.160
It’s a giant, high-speed, hyper-optimised
clearing-house:

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.920
growers deliver flowers
in the early morning,

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.320
and by lunchtime they’re sold
and on their way to buyers,

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.040
either via the warehouses next door
or in trucks that ship them onwards.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.920
So okay, it’s a logistics warehouse.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.480
The scale is impressive but
it’s nothing too special.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.200
The famous part is how the
flowers are sold there.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.480
If you’ve heard of a Dutch auction?
This is one of those auctions,

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:23.760
I think the biggest one anywhere.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.640
There’s lots of historical footage
of the big auction clock,

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.960
it used to be analog
and then went digital.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.080
Hundreds of flower buyers all sat
in theatre seating

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.400
as racks of flowers are paraded
in front of them.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.160
For each lot of flowers, the clock counts
from a high price to a low price.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.320
First to press their button
stops the clock

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.920
and pays whatever it’s showing.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.600
Press too late, someone else
gets the flowers.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.920
Press too early, you’ve paid too much.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.240
It's an amazing system,
it looks great on camera,

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.080
it’s been covered by loads of media
over the years. And… it’s gone.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.480
I thought the pandemic would
have caused some changes, but…

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.880
no, it turns out this all changed years ago.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.400
The internet enabled remote buying,

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.120
so the theatre seating got
emptier and emptier over the years.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.200
There’s now a reputation system
for the sellers,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.920
so buyers could know the quality
was as advertised

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.840
without actually inspecting
the flowers in person.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.880
By the mid-2010s, those racks of flowers weren’t
being pushed through the auction rooms,

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:15.920
and by the end of the decade,
the rooms were closed down.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.680
I got shown one of them, it looked
abandoned and actually a little bit sad.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.920
The replacement is many auctioneers
running many clocks, over the internet,

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:27.200
all at the same time,
sitting in cubicles with microphones.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.520
The flower auction isn’t like
some old railway

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.200
that gets kept alive
as a tourist attraction,

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.200
or preserved by a group of volunteers running
what's basically a reenactment society.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.160
It turns over billions of euros
every year,

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.360
and if tradition gets in the way of that,
then tradition has to go.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.600
And I think the sources aren’t
up to date because

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.760
“this famous thing has
slowly been replaced”

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.320
isn’t a nice friendly story.
And besides that:

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.080
60% of what goes
that warehouse

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.840
is sold in advance by contract now,
bypassing the clocks entirely.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.400
Some of the really large buyers,
like international supermarket chains,

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.600
are making private arrangements with
sellers who don’t use the warehouse,

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.520
because those chains are big enough

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.280
that they can handle the logistics
themselves and that saves money.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.400
The tourists do still visit.
The scale there is still impressive,

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.760
and I think it’s still worth
going to see

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.680
if you’ve got some spare time
near Amsterdam.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.280
But if you were hoping to watch
the famous Dutch Auction Clock,

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.520
well, these days it’s a lesson to
anyone who thinks that

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.680
they won’t need to change
with the times.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.200
In the business world, tradition is
rarely more important than money.

